---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-by-month`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D by Month of the Year',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D by Month', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D by Month: The Northern Hemisphere Year in UVB and 25(OH)D"
  description="A month-by-month walk through vitamin D availability in the Northern Hemisphere — from the January vitamin D winter to the June peak and back — with dosing implications."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-by-month", label: "By Month of Year" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D by Month of the Year</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Vitamin D production from sunlight follows a predictable annual cycle that most
      people underestimate. The cycle is largely driven by solar zenith angle — how
      steep the sun sits in the sky at noon. Latitudes above about 37° experience
      months when UVB simply doesn't reach the ground in synthesising quantity,
      regardless of how sunny the day feels.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">January — the deepest vitamin D winter</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Serum 25(OH)D reaches its annual minimum in mid-to-late January in the Northern
      Hemisphere. At latitudes above ~35° N, no meaningful vitamin D synthesis is
      occurring from sunlight — even at noon on a clear day. UVB is being absorbed by
      the thicker atmosphere the low-angle sun travels through. Supplementation is
      essential; 1,000–2,000 IU/day for most adults, higher if starting deficient.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">February — nadir continues</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Days are lengthening but sun angle is still low. In Boston (42° N), London (51° N),
      or Berlin (52° N), UVB remains below the synthesising threshold. The Mediterranean
      (~40° N) starts to see meaningful UVB at midday. Continue winter supplementation.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">March — the equinox reboot</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The spring equinox marks the return of synthesising UVB in most temperate zones.
      Sun angle reaches ~50° at noon. Fair-skinned adults can start making meaningful
      vitamin D from 15–30 minutes of face and forearm exposure at midday. Serum 25(OH)D
      hasn't caught up yet — there's a ~4–6 week lag between UVB return and rising
      blood levels.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">April — 25(OH)D starts climbing</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cross-sectional data (NHANES, UK Biobank) show serum 25(OH)D beginning meaningful
      rise in April. Fair-skinned adults spending time outdoors at midday can start
      reducing supplementation dose or frequency. Darker-skinned adults at high latitudes
      still benefit from continuing supplementation year-round.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">May — approaching peak synthesis</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Sun is high, days are long. This is when 25(OH)D rises fastest. Sunburn risk also
      climbs sharply — a few minutes' unaccustomed sun on unprotected skin can produce
      a first-degree burn. Aim for pre-erythemal exposure: enough to redden slightly
      but not burn.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">June — annual peak</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Summer solstice. Sun is at maximum altitude; solar noon delivers the year's peak
      UVB. 5–15 minutes of arm and leg exposure between 10 AM and 2 PM produces
      approximately the equivalent of 1,000–2,000 IU of oral vitamin D in fair skin —
      more in longer sessions or larger uncovered areas. Many people meet requirements
      from this alone; supplementation can be reduced or paused.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">July — sustained peak, deepening reserves</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Serum 25(OH)D peaks in late July / early August in the Northern Hemisphere — about
      6 weeks after the solstice, reflecting the pharmacokinetic delay. This is when
      adipose vitamin D stores are being replenished for the coming winter. See our
      <a href="/vitamin-d-reserves" class="text-amber-700 underline">reserves model</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">August — the pivot</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Serum 25(OH)D still near peak but UVB starts declining. Days shorten noticeably.
      This is the last month when unassisted sun exposure fully covers vitamin D needs
      in most people at high temperate latitudes.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">September — resumption of supplementation for many</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Autumn equinox. Days = nights. Sun angle drops rapidly. Above 37° N, UVB starts to
      lose reliability at midday. Serum 25(OH)D begins its 6-month decline. Time to
      restart or intensify supplementation.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">October — winter is coming</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      For most of North America and Europe, October marks the transition into
      vitamin D winter. Boston (42° N) sees its last meaningful synthesising day mid-
      October; London (51° N) around September 30. From here, sun exposure is dietary
      variety and mood benefit but not a vitamin D strategy.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">November — deep winter</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      UVB is essentially absent above 40° N. 25(OH)D falls at roughly 1–2 ng/mL per
      month. Supplement compliance now determines late-winter status.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">December — winter solstice</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Shortest days, lowest sun. 25(OH)D falling toward January nadir. Some traditional
      practices bear reflection: cod liver oil in Scandinavia through winter is not
      folklore — it's roughly 400–1,000 IU vitamin D per teaspoon plus vitamin A and
      omega-3, and predates commercial supplementation. Modern equivalent: 1,000–2,000
      IU/day cholecalciferol for adults, more if deficient.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Southern Hemisphere</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Everything above is offset by 6 months. December is the Southern Hemisphere's
      annual peak; June is deepest winter. Adjust accordingly for Australia, New Zealand,
      South Africa, and southern South America.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> These are population-scale patterns —
      individual variation is large. Use our <a href="/" class="text-amber-700 underline">calculator</a>
      for your latitude and lifestyle-specific estimate.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-by-month" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
